--  Building a pipeline, and deciding what becomes of what it wrote.
--
--  A pipeline is built a stage at a time with `pipe` and then run by one of the
--  commands that says what to do with its output. That is more words than a
--  `|` and it buys what a `|` cannot say: where the input comes from, which of
--  the two streams goes where, and whether the shell waits at all -- each as a
--  command a reader sees happen, rather than as punctuation whose meaning
--  depends on which side of it you are standing.
--
--  Each of the running forms empties the pipeline, so a script that wants two
--  of them builds it twice. That is why `pipe` appears before every one below.

--  A program every host has, reading its input and writing it sorted.
Sorter : String := "sort";

write_file ("beta" & Character'Val (10) & "alpha" & Character'Val (10),
            "unsorted.txt");

--  Where a pipeline reads from, said while it is being built. `pipe_from` runs
--  nothing: a pipeline that read one file and wrote another would otherwise be
--  two commands both insisting on running, and only one of them could.
pipe (Sorter);
pipe_from ("unsorted.txt");
pipe_into ("sorted.txt");

put_line ("into a file:");
put_line (Read_File ("sorted.txt"));

--  Or read back as a value, which is what `Output_Of` does for one program.
--  The pipeline is already built, so this takes no arguments.
pipe (Sorter);
pipe_from ("unsorted.txt");

put_line ("into a value: " & Output_Of_Pipe);

--  Everything it wrote, in the order it wrote it: what it said and what it
--  complained about, in one file. That is what a build log is, and two files
--  cannot be made into one however carefully a script interleaves them.
pipe (Sorter);
pipe_from ("unsorted.txt");
pipe_all_into ("everything.txt");

put_line ("both streams:");
put_line (Read_File ("everything.txt"));

--  Left running, the way `start` leaves one program running: it becomes a job
--  with a number, and `wait` is how a script comes back to it.
--
--  The number is three because the shell counts every job it has run, and the
--  two pipelines above were jobs as well -- waited for and forgotten, but
--  numbered on the way past. Neither `start` nor `pipe_start` takes a file, so
--  what this one writes goes where the shell's own output goes: before the
--  line after the wait, because the wait is what it waits for.
pipe (Sorter);
pipe_from ("unsorted.txt");
pipe_start;

wait (3);

put_line ("and that one ran while the script got on with something else");
